//
// A generic method whose receiver IS the type variable (`fn twice_of<T>(self: T)`) is refused at
// its declaration (`interfaces.md (G-Gen)`, loft#1539).
//
// A method is found on its receiver's type, and the definition is stored under the type
// variable's placeholder, `t_1T_twice_of` — a key no receiver can spell, so no call could reach
// it and each one reported an unknown field of whatever it was called on.  A receiver built over
// the variable (`self: vector<T>`) is keyed on `vector` and works: that half is the sibling
// guard `1539-a-generic-method-on-a-vector-receiver-instantiates.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: b12e15474 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1539b-a-generic-method-on-the-type-variable-itself-is-refused.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
//   CHANNEL: expectations, both backends.
//   ARMED: nothing.
//   WITNESS: on the control the refusal is not emitted (FAIL/1).
//   HOLDS: leak and panic are `none` on both trees.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a method's receiver cannot be the type variable T itself — a method is found on the receiver's type, and T is not a type until it is instantiated; write `fn twice_of<T>(x: T)` and call it as `twice_of(x)`

fn twice_of<T>(self: T) -> vector<T> { [self, self] }

fn main() {
  println("declared");
}
